```{r, include = FALSE}
knitr::opts_chunk$set(collapse = TRUE, comment = "#>", fig.width = 7, fig.height = 4.2, dpi = 72,
                      fig.path = "figures/sitar-", message = TRUE)
options(width = 100, tibble.width = 100, tibble.print_max = 12)
```

> **About this vignette.** All data are simulated with `wgh_simulate()`. The
> results show how the methods work and resemble the published patterns,
> but they are not the published findings and are not research results.
> SITAR models use at most 1,000 children each (development mode), the APHV bootstrap uses 10 replicates and the quantile bootstraps 50.

The pubertal growth spurt has a timing (age at peak height velocity, APHV),
an intensity (peak height velocity, PHV) and a duration. This vignette shows
how to estimate all three from school visits with SITAR models, and how to
relate them to prepubertal BMI. It reproduces the analyses of three studies:
the pubertal spurt by SITAR (Ho et al. 2026) [1], the distribution of
pubertal growth velocity (Ho 2026) [2] and the duration of the spurt
(Ho 2026) [3]. The vignette is precomputed because SITAR models take minutes
to fit.

```{r}
library(WeGrowHealthR)
wgh_config("dev")     # SITAR on up to 1,000 children per model, few bootstrap replicates
v <- wgh_simulate(30000, seed = 2026)$visits
```

## 1. Is there enough data at each age?

Mean curves are unreliable where few children are measured. `wgh_age_density()`
counts children and visits in half-year bins and reports the age at which
the number of children falls below 10% of its peak (`share`). The package
trims velocity curves at that age.

```{r}
ad <- wgh_age_density(v[v$age >= 6, ])
attr(ad, "sparse_from")
```

## 2. Fitting a SITAR model

SITAR (SuperImposition by Translation And Rotation) describes every child's
height curve as one mean curve shifted in size (`a`, cm), timing (`b`,
years, negative means earlier) and intensity (`c`, on the log scale,
positive means a faster, more intense spurt). `wgh_fit_sitar()` wraps
`sitar::sitar()`:

* `df` is the flexibility of the mean spline (4 or 5 is usual),
* `fixed` and `random` choose the effects (`"a + b + c"`, or
  `"a + b + c + d"` for the extended model),
* `age_range` the ages used and `min_visits` the visits needed per child,
* `max_children` limits the number of children (set by the run mode).

It never stops on a failed fit. Instead it keeps the error, so competing
models can be compared, and it retries once with one fewer degree of
freedom.

```{r}
boys <- v[v$sex == "Male" & v$age >= 6 & v$age <= 18, ]
fit_m <- wgh_fit_sitar(boys, df = 5, age_range = c(6, 18))
fit_m
```

The printout shows the number of children and visits, the population APHV
and PHV, and diagnostics: whether the fit converged, whether the velocity
curve turns negative in the tails, and the residual SD.

```{r sitar-both, fig.alt = "Mean height and velocity curves of boys from SITAR", fig.height = 3.8}
plot(fit_m, type = "both", line_width = 1.2)
```

`plot()` takes `type = "velocity"`, `"height"` or `"both"`, `trim` and
`show_data`, plus the shared display options.

### Checking the model

Two checks matter before using the estimates. `wgh_check_identifiable()`
flags random effects that are almost perfectly correlated, a sign that the
model cannot separate them. `wgh_sitar_select()` compares candidate
specifications by BIC, a positive velocity in the tails and stable APHV and
PHV, and picks one by a stated rule (the procedure used for girls in [1]).

```{r}
wgh_check_identifiable(fit_m)
girls <- v[v$sex == "Female" & v$age >= 6 & v$age <= 16, ]
sel <- wgh_sitar_select(girls)
sel$table
sel$selected
sel$reason
fit_f <- sel$fits[[sel$selected]]
```

Read `sel$reason` before using the selected model. Here, with a subsample
of 1,000 girls in development mode, no candidate meets every criterion, so
the function keeps the first model and says why each candidate failed. With
the full cohort (`wgh_config("publication")`) more candidates may
converge. When none is acceptable, show the mean curve only over the ages
with enough data and report the reason with the results.

## 3. APHV and PHV with honest intervals

`wgh_aphv()` gives the population APHV and PHV from the mean curve.
`wgh_sitar_ci()` adds intervals. The default `method = "bootstrap"`
resamples children and refits the model `B` times, which carries all the
uncertainty of the curve. `method = "legacy"` reproduces the interval of the
original analysis code (the estimate plus or minus 1.96 times the SD of
individual APHV over the square root of n). It is not a bootstrap: it treats
the spread of individual timing as sampling error, so its width has no
clear meaning, and it is kept only to match published numbers.

```{r}
ci_m <- wgh_sitar_ci(fit_m, B = 10)
ci_m
wgh_sitar_ci(fit_m, method = "legacy")
```

With 1,000 children the population curve is estimated precisely, so both
intervals are narrow here. With `B = 10` the bootstrap interval is rough. Use
`B = 200` or more for a paper (`wgh_config("publication")` does that).

## 4. Individual timing and intensity

`wgh_sitar_effects()` returns one row per child with the random effects and
the child's own APHV and PHV. The individual APHV uses the exact SITAR
formula, not an approximation. `early_timing` flags children whose timing is
earlier than the population (`b < early_cut`), and `faster_tempo` children
with a more intense spurt (`c > 0`).

```{r}
eff_m <- wgh_sitar_effects(fit_m)
eff_f <- wgh_sitar_effects(fit_f)
head(eff_m)
summary(eff_m$aphv)
summary(eff_f$aphv)
```

Girls reach their peak years before boys, the most consistent finding of
growth studies, and individual APHV varies around the population value, more
in girls than in boys.

**Next step.** Join these effects to other data by `id`, for example to ask
whether prepubertal BMI predicts earlier timing (section 7).

## 5. Velocity curves of individual children

`wgh_velocity_panel()` evaluates each child's velocity curve on a grid,
either by age (`time = "age"`) or by years from the child's own APHV
(`time = "aphv"`). `observed_only = TRUE` keeps only the part of each curve
covered by the child's visits, so no child is extrapolated.

```{r}
pan <- wgh_velocity_panel(fit_m, time = "aphv", grid = seq(-4, 4, by = 0.05), observed_only = FALSE)
head(pan)
```

## 6. Duration of the spurt

`wgh_curve_features()` measures the width of each child's velocity peak.
The default (`reference = "takeoff"`) measures the width at 25%, 50% and 75%
of the spurt amplitude, that is, the rise from the take-off velocity to the
peak. Older code measured the width at a fraction of PHV
(`reference = "zero"`), but the velocity of most children never falls to
half of PHV before the data start, so that width is left-censored for most
of them. The package reports censoring so this cannot go unnoticed.

```{r}
cf <- wgh_curve_features(pan)
summary(cf$width_p50)
pan_f <- wgh_velocity_panel(fit_f, time = "aphv", grid = seq(-4, 4, by = 0.05), observed_only = FALSE)
censored <- function(p) c(amplitude = mean(wgh_curve_features(p)$censored_left_p50),
  fraction_of_phv = mean(wgh_curve_features(p, thresholds = 0.5, reference = "zero")$censored_left_p50))
rbind(boys = censored(pan), girls = censored(pan_f))
```

`width_p50` is the width of the spurt in years at half its amplitude. The
second table gives the share of children whose width cannot be measured
because the velocity curve does not fall far enough before the data start.
Girls start their spurt early, so with the legacy definition the width is
censored for most of them, while the amplitude definition measures every
child.

## 7. Does prepubertal BMI change the spurt?

The prepubertal exposure is the earliest BAZ from 5 years at or before 11
years in boys and 9 years in girls (see `vignette("trajectories")`).

```{r}
base <- wgh_index_exposure(v, "baz", name = "pre")
base <- base[!duplicated(base$id), c("id", "pre_value")]
names(base)[2] <- "baz_pre"
e <- merge(eff_m, base, by = "id")
summary(lm(b ~ baz_pre, data = e))$coefficients
```

A negative slope means higher prepubertal BMI goes with earlier timing.

The second study asked whether the association is the same across the
distribution. `wgh_scalar_quantiles()` fits quantile regressions of a
child-level outcome with a bootstrap over children, and reports the OLS
estimate alongside:

```{r}
wgh_scalar_quantiles(e, b ~ baz_pre, "baz_pre", taus = c(0.1, 0.25, 0.5, 0.75, 0.9), B = 50)
```

`wgh_mediation()` asks how much of the association of BMI with PHV passes
through earlier timing:

```{r}
wgh_mediation(e, "baz_pre", "b", "phv", B = 50)
```

The total effect is the change in PHV per unit of prepubertal BAZ. The
natural indirect effect is the part that passes through timing `b`, and the
natural direct effect the rest. A proportion mediated near or slightly above
1 means that essentially all of the association works through earlier
timing (a proportion above 1 occurs when the direct effect is slightly
negative).

## 8. The three studies in one call each

`wgh_flow_pubertal_spurt()` runs the age-density check, the male model, the
female model selection, identifiability checks, APHV and PHV with intervals,
individual effects and the links between prepubertal anthropometry and
timing. Its report is then passed as `spurt` to the velocity and duration
flows, so the SITAR models are fitted only once.

```{r}
spurt <- wgh_flow_pubertal_spurt(v)
spurt
spurt$tables$aphv_phv_population
spurt$tables$early_timing_logistic
```

```{r flow-velocity, fig.alt = "Mean velocity curves of boys and girls with APHV marked"}
plot(spurt, "fig_velocity")
```

```{r flow-aphv-bmi, fig.alt = "Individual APHV by prepubertal BMI category", fig.height = 3.8}
plot(spurt, "fig_aphv_by_bmi")
```

```{r}
velocity <- wgh_flow_velocity_distribution(v, spurt = spurt)
velocity$tables$scalar_quantiles
duration <- wgh_flow_spurt_duration(v, spurt = spurt)
duration$tables$censoring
duration$tables$duration_ols
```

```{r flow-velocity-slopes, fig.alt = "Change in velocity per BAZ unit across velocity quantiles", fig.height = 3.8}
plot(velocity, "fig_velocity_slopes")
```

```{r flow-duration, fig.alt = "Spurt duration by prepubertal BAZ", fig.height = 3.8}
plot(duration, "fig_duration_by_bmi", alpha = 0.4)
```

## Where to go next

* The individual SITAR effects are features for prediction
  (`vignette("prediction")`) and growth phenotypes
  (`vignette("phenotypes-states")`). Size `a` is estimated from the late
  visits, so it must not be used to predict late height. The package never
  joins it.
* `wgh_fpca_curves()` decomposes the velocity curves into their main shapes.

## References

```{r, echo = FALSE, results = "asis"}
cat(paste0(wgh_references(c("spurt", "velocity", "duration"), format = "text"), collapse = "\n\n"))
```
